endency structure: the foundational models and DTOs have no dependencies, then the configuration and infrastructure layer builds on those, followed by service and mapper implementations, and finally the controller and unit tests at the top. This gives me a clear progression where each group only depends on what came before.\n\nI'm calculating the test coverage ratio to make sure I'm hitting at least 70% test items. Currently I'm at 64% with the test infrastructure counted, but dropping below 60% if I exclude it. I need to add more test items to reach the minimum threshold—looks like I should include security integration tests to cover the authentication endpoints and error scenarios.\n\nBut stepping back, 14 implementation items feels excessive for a straightforward CRUD application, especially when many of these are trivially small or boilerplate. I should consolidate items where it makes sense—the real constraint is whether a single agent session can reasonably complete one item in one go, and multiple small classes can definitely be handled together.\n\nSo I'm reorganizing into a consolidated structure: domain models and DTOs as one item, database schema and test resources as another, then grouping the configuration classes together, followed by the mapper and validator, then the service layer, controller, and finally the test suites. This brings the total down to a more manageable count while keeping dependencies clear and respecting the P0/P1 priority levels.\n\nNow I'm questioning whether test items should live in the same dependency groups as their implementation counterparts, or if they should form their own separate grouping structure.\n\nLooking at the dependencies more carefully, test items are tied to both the code they're testing and the test infrastructure itself. So model and DTO tests depend on items 1, 2, and 8, mapper tests on items 6 and 8, validator tests on items 7 and 8, service tests on item 9, exception handler tests on item 10, controller tests on item 11, and security tests on items 5 and 8. This suggests most of the earlier test items could actually slot into Group 3 alongside their implementation dependencies, while service and exception handler tests would go to Group 4, and controller tests to Group 5.\n\nBut I'm overcomplicating this. The real insight is that tests should go right after their corresponding implementation items for better scheduling. So I'm restructuring the whole plan into a cleaner dependency hierarchy where Group 1 covers the foundational domain and DTO models, Group 2 adds the configuration and utility layers, and Group 3 will contain the service and mapper implementations with their tests bundled together. Item 9: EmployeeService (interface + impl) - M\nItem 10: GlobalExceptionHandler - S\nItem 11: Model & DTO unit tests - S\nItem 12: EmployeeMapper integration tests - M\nItem 13: EmployeeValidator unit tests - S\nItem 14: Security integration tests - S\n\n**Group 4 (P1, depends on Group 3):**\nItem 15: EmployeeService unit tests - M\nItem 16: EmployeeController - M\n\n**Group 5 (P1, depends on Group 4):**\nItem 17: Controller integration tests - CRUD operations - M\nItem 18: Controller integration tests - Search & pagination - M\nItem 19: Controller integration tests - Auth & error handling - M\n\nImplementation items: 1-10, 16 = 11\nTest items: 8, 11-15, 17-19 = 9\n9/11 = 82% ✓\nTotal: 19 items\n\nNow I'm reviewing the spec more carefully to make sure I've captured all the required mapper methods, service operations, and controller endpoints correctly.\n\nI see the controller needs to handle the complete REST API surface including the search endpoint with query parameters and proper HTTP status codes for different scenarios. The service layer needs to validate for duplicate emails during create and update operations using the mapper's findByEmail method. I have enough detail now to write comprehensive acceptance criteria for each item.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"